FINAL ANSWER:
Yes, two people appeared under more than one identity. I wrote both fixes to a new `.mailmap` file. Both are guesses from names and addresses until you confirm them.

**Duplicates found**
- **Troy Goode** used `<email redacted>` (93 commits) and `troy@laptop.example` (2 commits). The name is the same on both addresses.
- **Alejandro Caravaca Puchades** used the same address, `<email redacted>`, under two names: the full name (4 commits) and `alex94puchades` (1 commit).

**What I changed**
- I created `.mailmap` at the repo root. It has a comment line saying the entries are proposals to confirm, then these two lines:
  ```
  Troy Goode <<email redacted>> <troy@laptop.example>
  Alejandro Caravaca Puchades <<email redacted>> alex94puchades <<email redacted>>
  ```
- Nothing is committed. Git, blame and the counting tool all read this file, so every count now merges these identities.

**New top author: Troy Goode, by surviving lines**
- Troy Goode: 810 lines (40.8%) and 95 commits (30.2%). Before the merge, the gmail address alone had 762 lines (38.4%).
- Douglas Christopher Wilson is second with 629 lines (31.7%). He has more commits than anyone (143, 45.4%), so he is the top author by commit count.
- Counting lines this way, Troy Goode and Douglas Christopher Wilson together hold 72.5% of the 1,985 surviving lines, so the bus factor is 2. The bus factor here is the smallest number of authors holding at least 50% of the surviving lines. Reaching 80% takes 4 authors. Both figures cover the whole repository.

**Left out of the numbers**
- Commit `62628c0`, "style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes" by Fiona Formatter, is ignored. It only changed whitespace and quotes. Counting it would give Fiona 291 lines (14.7%) and Troy Goode 715 (36.0%). He would still be the top author.
- One bot identity is excluded: 10 lines and 11 commits.

The line counts don't measure code quality, review work, or code that was deleted on purpose.

